  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a global supply chain?",
      "a": "A global supply chain is all the steps and places involved in making and delivering a product, from mines and designers to factories, ships and shops, spread across many countries.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a supply chain?",
        "Where are phones made?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is a phone made in so many countries?",
      "a": "Each place specialises in what it does best or cheapest: designers in the USA and South Korea, chip factories in Taiwan, skilled assembly in China, India and Vietnam, and metals wherever the rocks hold them.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are phones put together in China?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do the metals in a phone come from?",
      "a": "A phone holds dozens of elements. Cobalt comes mostly from DR Congo, lithium from Australia and Chile, copper from Chile, tin from Indonesia, and rare earth metals mostly from China.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the cobalt in batteries come from?",
        "What is cobalt used for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are so many chips made in Taiwan?",
      "a": "Taiwan is home to TSMC, the world’s biggest maker of chips for other companies. It makes most of the world’s most advanced chips, used in phones, computers and cars.",
      "asks": [
        "What is TSMC?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens when a supply chain breaks?",
      "a": "If one step stops, everything after it waits. In 2020 and 2021 the pandemic caused a chip shortage that slowed car factories and made game consoles hard to buy.",
      "asks": [
        "What was the chip shortage?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Democratic Republic of the Congo produces about 70% of the world's cobalt.",
    "Taiwan's TSMC makes the large majority of the world's most advanced computer chips.",
    "A smartphone contains dozens of different chemical elements."
